; =====================================================================
; figure-5-1-avstring.derived.abnf
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. Written for this library from NISTIR 7695
; (CPE Naming Specification Version 2.3, August 2011), section 5.3.2
; and Figure 5-1, with rules from 5.1, 5.3.1, 5.3.3.1 and 5.3.3.10.
; The verbatim figure is figure-5-1-avstring.abnf beside this file.
; Never cite a rule from this file as the specification's own grammar,
; except where a comment says the rule is the figure's rule unchanged.
;
; Language: ABNF, RFC 5234 (the figure cites RFC 2234; the syntax used
; here is the same in both). ASCII only. Checked by
; ../examples/run-fixtures.derived.py, which parses this file with a
; strict RFC 5234 parser and compiles each root rule to a regular
; expression.
;
; Source PDF SHA-256
; 01553a4638b21eda10018690b71162e91605d78eb15850f881bd0b2b691ac3f6
; <https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/Legacy/IR/nistir7695.pdf>
;
; Conventions. Numbers such as 5.3.2 are NISTIR 7695 section numbers.
; Text in double quotes that a comment attributes to a section is
; verbatim from the specification (a sentence the PDF wraps across
; lines is joined). Everything else in the comments is the library's.
;
; ROOT RULES
;
;   avstring         Figure 5-1 unchanged except one rename (see PART 1).
;   avstring-prose   The prose of 5.3.2, items 1 to 6, as a grammar
;                    (the library's reading; see PART 2).
;   avstring-strict  Strings that both avstring and avstring-prose
;                    accept (PART 3). Use this one to validate a value.
;   part-string      The three string values 5.3.3.1 allows for part.
;   language-string  A language value: an RFC 5646 Language-Tag written
;                    with each hyphen quoted, as 5.3.2 item 5 requires.
;   wfn-notation     The "wfn:[...]" illustration notation of 5.1, as
;                    the examples print it (PART 5).
;
; Where avstring and avstring-prose differ (exactly two families of
; strings, PART 3) the specification contradicts itself; README.md
; beside this file records both readings as findings.
; =====================================================================

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PART 1. Figure 5-1, made valid RFC 5234 ABNF.
;
; The only change from the printed figure: the rule name spec_chrs is
; written spec-chrs. RFC 2234 section 2.1 (and RFC 5234 section 2.1)
; allows only letters, digits and hyphens in a rule name. DQUOTE, ALPHA
; and DIGIT keep the figure's definitions, which equal the RFC 5234
; core rules. Every other line is the figure's, token for token.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

avstring          = ( body / ( spec-chrs *body2 )) *1spec-chrs
spec-chrs         = 1*spec1 / spec2
spec1             = "?"
spec2             = "*"
body              = ( body1 *body2 ) / 2*body2
body1             = unreserved / quoted1
body2             = unreserved / quoted2
unreserved        = ALPHA / DIGIT / "_"
quoted1           = escape (escape / special / punc-no-dash)
quoted2           = escape (escape / special / punc-w-dash)
escape            = "\"
special           = spec1 / spec2
dash              = "-"
punc-no-dash      = "!" / DQUOTE / "#"/ "$"/ "%" / "&" / "'" /
                    "(" / ")" / "+" / "," / "." / "/" /
                    ":" / ";" / "<" / "=" / ">" / "@" / "[" /
                    "]" / "^" / "`" / "{" / "|" / "}" / "~"
punc-w-dash       = punc-no-dash / dash
DQUOTE            = %x22 ; double quote
ALPHA             = %x41-5a / %x61-7A    ; A-Z or a-z
DIGIT             = %x30-39    ; 0-9

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PART 2. The prose of 5.3.2 as a grammar (library reading).
;
; 5.3.2 says: "Value strings assigned to attributes of WFNs SHALL be
; non-empty contiguous strings of bytes encoded using printable Unicode
; Transformation Format-8 (UTF-8) characters with hexadecimal values
; between x00 and x7F [RFC3629]." Items 1 to 6 then admit letters and
; digits (1), the underscore, with no whitespace (2), the backslash as
; escape character (3), the special characters "*" and "?" (4), every
; other printable non-alphanumeric character only when quoted (5), and
; forbid a quoted hyphen by itself (6). Item 4 says:
;   "A special character MAY be included at the beginning of an
;    otherwise non-empty value string, and/or at the end of the
;    string. A special character MUST NOT be embedded within a value
;    string. A single asterisk MUST NOT be used by itself as an
;    attribute value. The asterisk MUST NOT be used more than once in
;    sequence. A single question mark MAY be used by itself as an
;    attribute value. The question mark MAY be used more than once in
;    sequence."
;
; Readings (judgement calls, listed in README.md):
;   P1  A leading or trailing group of special characters is either a
;       run of question marks or one asterisk ("more than once in
;       sequence" is allowed for "?" only); a mixed group such as "?*"
;       counts as an embedded special character.
;   P2  A value made only of special characters is allowed only when it
;       is one or more question marks ("A single question mark MAY be
;       used by itself"; "more than once in sequence"); "*", "**", "?*"
;       and "*?" are not allowed ("otherwise non-empty").
;   P3  Item 6 forbids "\-" only as the whole value; "\-?" and "?\-"
;       are allowed (the quoted hyphen is then not "by itself").
;   P4  The prose does not say whether a letter, digit or underscore
;       may follow the escape character. This rule follows Figure 5-1:
;       it may not (quoted1, quoted2).
;
; Under these readings the prose is the regular language below. body
; is reused from PART 1: one or more units (an unreserved character or
; an escape pair) that are not exactly one quoted hyphen.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

avstring-prose    = 1*spec1                        ; P2: "?", "??", ...
                  / body                           ; no special character
                  / spec-chrs 1*body2 [ spec-chrs ]  ; leading group
                  / 1*body2 spec-chrs              ; trailing group only

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PART 3. Strings both readings accept.
;
; ABNF has no intersection operator, so the intersection of avstring
; (PART 1) and avstring-prose (PART 2) is written out. The two differ in
; exactly two families of strings:
;   D1  Figure 5-1 accepts and the prose forbids: values made only of
;       special characters other than question-mark runs: "*", "**",
;       "*?", "*??", "?*", "??*" (spec-chrs followed by zero body2 and
;       an optional second spec-chrs).
;   D2  The prose allows and Figure 5-1 rejects: one quoted hyphen
;       followed by a trailing group: "\-?", "\-??", "\-*". (Figure
;       5-1's body excludes a lone "\-" even when special characters
;       follow it, but its second alternative admits "?\-".)
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

avstring-strict   = 1*spec1
                  / body [ spec-chrs ]
                  / spec-chrs 1*body2 [ spec-chrs ]

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PART 4. Per-attribute value restrictions (5.3.3).
;
; 5.3.3.1 says: "The part attribute SHALL have one of these three
; string values:" "a", "o" and "h". The values are written as %x so
; that they are case-sensitive (an RFC 5234 quoted string is not).
; Reading: the logical values ANY and NA stay allowed for part (5.2
; item 2 defaults an unspecified attribute to ANY; Figure 6-1 allows an
; empty part; Figure 6-3 allows a logical part).
;
; 5.3.3.10 says: "Values for this attribute SHALL be valid language
; tags as defined by [RFC5646]". In a WFN the hyphens of the tag are
; quoted (5.3.2 item 5), so "en-us" is written "en\-us". The rule is a
; prose value: RFC 5646 is not reproduced here. run-fixtures.derived.py
; checks the RFC 5646 section 2.1 Language-Tag production (RFC 5646
; "well-formed"); RFC 5646 "valid" also needs the IANA subtag registry,
; which nothing here checks.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

part-string       = %x61 / %x6F / %x68    ; a / o / h

language-string   = <RFC 5646 Language-Tag, each "-" written as "\-">

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; PART 5. The WFN illustration notation (5.1, 5.3.1, 5.3.2).
;
; 5.1 says WFNs "will be notated as lists of attribute-value pairs
; enclosed in square brackets", prefixed with the string wfn:, and
; that "This notation is used solely for the purposes of explaining and
; illustrating the concepts and procedures specified herein." 5.3.1:
; logical values are "written in all uppercase characters, without
; surrounding quotation marks". 5.3.2: string values "will be enclosed
; in double quotes". The rule below is the library's, written so the
; examples can be parsed; it is not a data format (5.1: "The WFN is a
; logical construct only."). notation-string accepts any printable
; ASCII with backslash escapes, so that a value can be read first and
; judged by avstring-strict afterwards. Attribute names are quoted
; strings and so match in any case, as 5.1 requires ("Lexical case
; SHALL NOT distinguish attributes from one another"). The examples
; print no space after a comma; the 5.1 template prints one ("a1=v1,
; a2=v2"), so spaces are allowed there.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------

wfn-notation      = "wfn:[" [ av-pair *( "," *SP av-pair ) ] "]"
av-pair           = attribute "=" ( DQUOTE notation-string DQUOTE
                                    / logical-value )
attribute         = "part" / "vendor" / "product" / "version" /
                    "update" / "edition" / "language" / "sw_edition" /
                    "target_sw" / "target_hw" / "other"
logical-value     = %x41.4E.59 / %x4E.41  ; ANY / NA, upper case only
notation-string   = *( %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7E / escape %x20-7E )
SP                = %x20
